# Working with Alec on this project

Alec is a non-programmer who works experientially: try something, observe the
result, change it — not plan-first, spec-first engineering. He debugs well by
feel but doesn't yet have formal engineering vocabulary. Treat every session as
partly a teaching session, not just a delivery pipeline. He wants to end up
able to read and reason about this code himself, not just receive working code.

## Comment for design intent, not just mechanics

Don't write comments that just restate the code ("loops through items"). Write
comments that capture *why* — what design or product decision this line
exists to satisfy.

This project already does this well for game/randomizer logic: see
prompt-generator.js, where comments cite rule IDs (R2, R4, R6...) that map to
RANDOMIZER_LOGIC_SPEC.md. Extend the same pattern to visual/UX decisions using
DESIGN_DECISIONS.md and D-numbered tags (D1, D2...). Any time you add or change
a color, spacing value, timing, or interaction rule that a designer chose on
purpose, add or update a row in DESIGN_DECISIONS.md and reference its tag in
the comment at the code site — not just in CHANGELOG.txt.

## Externalize the knobs

Any value likely to get hand-tuned by trial and error — colors, spacing,
timing thresholds, copy — belongs in one clearly named place (CSS custom
properties, a constants object at the top of a module) instead of scattered
as inline magic numbers. Default to adding new tunables there. If you find an
existing bare magic number while working nearby, it's fine to flag it, but
don't do a drive-by refactor of unrelated code.

## Audit passes, not continuous nagging

Iterative/experiential work naturally accumulates dead branches, duplicate
logic, and stale debug logging — that's a normal cost of this way of working,
not a mistake to fix mid-flow. Don't interrupt normal iteration with cleanup
suggestions. Instead, when Alec explicitly asks for an audit (or roughly every
10-ish feature changes), do a dedicated pass:
- List functions/files that look unreferenced.
- Flag places where CHANGELOG.txt's claims (line counts, "removed" features)
  don't match what's actually in the code.
- Flag console.log/debugLog lines that look like leftover debugging rather
  than a real diagnostic someone would want to keep.
Propose removals as a list for Alec to confirm — never silently delete.

## Teach as you go

When you introduce a term or pattern he hasn't used before (e.g. "debounce",
"state", "closure", "DOM"), give a one-line plain-language definition tied to
the actual code you just wrote, not an abstract tutorial aside. This is how
his vocabulary should build over time.

## Keep CHANGELOG.txt honest

If a change supersedes or removes something already logged, update or note
against that earlier entry — don't just append a new entry and leave the old
one contradicting the current code. (Current example needing a decision: a
"THEME SYSTEM REMOVAL" entry exists, but theme-manager.js is still loaded in
every HTML file — resolve which is true and fix the doc.)
